93: /**
94:  * A single execution of a vertex. While an {@link ExecutionVertex} can be executed multiple times
95:  * (for recovery, re-computation, re-configuration), this class tracks the state of a single
96:  * execution of that vertex and the resources.
97:  *
98:  * <h2>Lock free state transitions</h2>
99:  *
100:  * <p>In several points of the code, we need to deal with possible concurrent state changes and
101:  * actions. For example, while the call to deploy a task (send it to the TaskManager) happens, the
102:  * task gets cancelled.
103:  *
104:  * <p>We could lock the entire portion of the code (decision to deploy, deploy, set state to
105:  * running) such that it is guaranteed that any "cancel command" will only pick up after deployment
106:  * is done and that the "cancel command" call will never overtake the deploying call.
107:  *
108:  * <p>This blocks the threads big time, because the remote calls may take long. Depending of their
109:  * locking behavior, it may even result in distributed deadlocks (unless carefully avoided). We
110:  * therefore use atomic state updates and occasional double-checking to ensure that the state after
111:  * a completed call is as expected, and trigger correcting actions if it is not. Many actions are
112:  * also idempotent (like canceling).
113:  */
