46: /**
47:  * A {@code Transformation} represents the operation that creates a DataStream. Every DataStream has
48:  * an underlying {@code Transformation} that is the origin of said DataStream.
49:  *
50:  * <p>API operations such as DataStream#map create a tree of {@code Transformation}s underneath.
51:  * When the stream program is to be executed this graph is translated to a StreamGraph using
52:  * StreamGraphGenerator.
53:  *
54:  * <p>A {@code Transformation} does not necessarily correspond to a physical operation at runtime.
55:  * Some operations are only logical concepts. Examples of this are union, split/select data stream,
56:  * partitioning.
57:  *
58:  * <p>The following graph of {@code Transformations}:
59:  *
60:  * <pre>{@code
61:  *   Source              Source
62:  *      +                   +
63:  *      |                   |
64:  *      v                   v
65:  *  Rebalance          HashPartition
66:  *      +                   +
67:  *      |                   |
68:  *      |                   |
69:  *      +------>Union<------+
70:  *                +
71:  *                |
72:  *                v
73:  *              Split
74:  *                +
75:  *                |
76:  *                v
77:  *              Select
78:  *                +
79:  *                v
80:  *               Map
81:  *                +
82:  *                |
83:  *                v
84:  *              Sink
85:  * }</pre>
86:  *
87:  * <p>Would result in this graph of operations at runtime:
88:  *
89:  * <pre>{@code
90:  * Source              Source
91:  *   +                   +
92:  *   |                   |
93:  *   |                   |
94:  *   +------->Map<-------+
95:  *             +
96:  *             |
97:  *             v
98:  *            Sink
